#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216979 :question/id #uuid "b32d0028-8e5c-5f8a-b989-a7ceff5c1fc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-20896" :question/problem "Show that $I(x) = \\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ is an integrating factor of the equation \n\n$$\n(x^2y-y^3) \\,\\textrm{d}x - x^{3} \\,\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y] = 0\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x{y}^{3}}(({x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\, \\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y^2}-\\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad Q(x,y) =- \\dfrac{x^2}{y^3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{is}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x)=\\dfrac{1}{xy^3}$ $\\text{is}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216980 :question/id #uuid "106cd4dd-2f3f-541e-899a-e2776a39e382" :question/math-academy-id "e-20898" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n\\left(x + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}x + \\left(xy + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x+y^2,\\qquad Q(x,y)=xy+\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(x)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216981 :question/id #uuid "b69fa09b-2661-5edc-8fcb-c29120e56325" :question/math-academy-id "e-20935" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nx\\,y' = 4y - 3x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x) = \\exp\\left({\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nFirst, we rewrite our equation in differential form: \n\n$$\n(3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x  + (x) \\;\\textrm{d} y  =0\n$$\n\nLet's denote \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = 3x - 4y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{x}(-4-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -\\frac{5}{x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|) \\\\  & ={x}^{-5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(x) \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) =  I(x)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx^{-5} \\cdot \\left((3x - 4y) \\;\\textrm{d}x + x\\;\\textrm{d} y\\right) = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217002 :question/id #uuid "294fc38f-1d72-52ed-a11d-d1003fb1b1bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-21772" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y = 0, \\qquad y > 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y) \\,\\textrm{d} x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{P} \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} - \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right) = h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y) = \\exp\\left({-\\int h(y)\\,\\textrm d y}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(y)$ denotes the exponential function $e^y.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =  y^2, \\qquad Q(x,y) =  xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(2y-y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int -({y}^{-1})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot \\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy\\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y^{-1}\\left(y^2 \\; \\textrm{d}x + xy \\; \\textrm{d}y \\right) = 0}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bebf3ee1-a32c-5e61-9975-024f91d5bed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216442} {:answer/id #uuid "e18a5f0a-3f6d-55f7-afe2-895eab73d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216443} {:answer/id #uuid "e9eb8eae-e363-5786-9572-28d9358c0b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216444} {:answer/id #uuid "0faf94ca-3c82-5981-b5de-40b67c49785b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} - \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216445}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d0254544-5cdd-5db1-9ac1-d0e9a67ff04d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04061054-cd88-55e6-bdeb-8266d51d37de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\"\\exp (g(x))\", \"\\exp (-g(x))\"" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216447} {:answer/id #uuid "2a53f1e7-a17f-5c20-8a8d-7ab29c0297f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216448} {:answer/id #uuid "7a262833-09c8-57fa-b594-245e4631bb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\exp (-\\intg(x)dx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216449}] :answer-field/id #uuid "32eaf4e4-ae7b-54e3-a4b8-7040c1b54ab1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186216446}] :question/id #uuid "02a15c04-b231-5e46-af80-834c2e1d6bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-318659" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n$y^{′} = 2xy - x$\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n$P(x, y)dx + Q(x, y)dy = 0$,\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y} + \\frac{∂Q}{∂x})\n$$ {{field-1}}\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, then an integrating factor for the equation is\n$\\exp (\\intg(x)dx)$ {{field-2}}\nwhere $\\exp (x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^{x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76982e4e-94cc-549d-90d0-cbc4cd181b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-1}{e}^{-1/y}\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298068} {:answer/id #uuid "873c8f9b-06ff-5065-a997-adc2ca7313b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/y}\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298139} {:answer/id #uuid "93702475-09c4-521e-b55a-05efbed4a749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1/y}\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "873c8f9b-06ff-5065-a997-adc2ca7313b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/y}\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298139} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dda93c0-f8e