b/ref 17592186309798} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a58c5c-e532-561e-b9bd-b42bfda6b620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8636a482-da7c-53f4-b210-d3375a6a85f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "isn't" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309798} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb48e22-5055-593a-8692-5c588dc1a00c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e2b872a-4e35-5059-b33b-25da543bae2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337259" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo show that $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ is an integrating factor for the given differential equation, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let's denote\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\,{{field-2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\,{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A function $I(x,y)$ is an integrating factor for the differential equation\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nif the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\nI(x,y)[P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y]=0.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to check if the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}({x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x) & =0 \\\\ -y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nFor our transformed equation, we have  \n\n$$\nP(x,y)=-y,\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\right)=\\frac{2x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation $\\text{isn't}$ exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(y)=\\frac{1}{y}$ $\\text{isn't}$ an integrating factor for the initial differential equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14fe2ea9-ad09-5630-af64-22131020c4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308904} {:answer/id #uuid "84dd9d79-839b-52a2-b19f-2d5cbfbb46f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309783} {:answer/id #uuid "96485065-3293-5ce0-ab20-2103330aa021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309951} {:answer/id #uuid "f828c22e-b382-55e9-826b-56e8a50f3e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14fe2ea9-ad09-5630-af64-22131020c4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308904} :answer-field/id #uuid "32615465-7010-53c6-8614-e7498764c988" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218