b0c0-248f0432c6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680913} {:answer/id #uuid "3f37e374-9773-5cdd-8079-a9be0f2e2728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680937} {:answer/id #uuid "6daf1bf9-904d-5425-9fb1-be4b3ab27105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680950} {:answer/id #uuid "967d5140-dfdb-54d3-b555-94c47c0bd755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6daf1bf9-904d-5425-9fb1-be4b3ab27105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680950} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee10b007-5365-54b6-9d90-f8fb46cc295b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9081b6a0-c7d3-5990-947e-4a1b371a14dc" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an **integrating factor** to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'=- 2 x y + 3 x\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Take $x$ to be real.\n\nFor a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes $e^x$.\n\nFirst, rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2 x y - 3 x,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nNow compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}}\n$$\n\nand multiplication gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$.\n\nHere $\\exp(x)=e^x$.\n\nFirst, rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n(2 x y - 3 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2 x y - 3 x,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2 x y - 3 x)=2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})&=\\frac{1}{1}(2 x-(0)) \\\\ &=2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a function of $x$ only. Choose the multiplicative constant to be one. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)&=\\exp(\\int (2 x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ &=\\exp(x^{2}) \\\\ &=e^{x^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the original differential equation by this integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)((2 x y - 3 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=I(x)\\cdot 0 \\\\ e^{x^{2}}((2 x y - 3 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186680980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "663546e0-af2a-50bb-9db0-2974475dca61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680948} {:answer/id #uuid "7d491