" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186680998} {:answer/id #uuid "e39012c8-31f9-5fcc-acd1-50bc92f84a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e39012c8-31f9-5fcc-acd1-50bc92f84a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681002} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9eee95-5358-5b96-b789-9a38aa301139" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6199880-c2c8-5909-977b-3c02880d20cc" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an **integrating factor** to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n(- 2 x y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Take $x$ to be real.\n\nFor a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes $e^x$.\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=- 2 x y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nNow compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand multiplication gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$.\n\nHere $\\exp(x)=e^x$.\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=- 2 x y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- 2 x y)=- 2 x \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})&=\\frac{1}{1}(- 2 x-(0)) \\\\ &=- 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a function of $x$ only. Choose the multiplicative constant to be one. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)&=\\exp(\\int (- 2 x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ &=\\exp(- x^{2}) \\\\ &=e^{- x^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the original differential equation by this integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)((- 2 x y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=I(x)\\cdot 0 \\\\ e^{- x^{2}}((- 2 x y)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a4d3a9e-6de9-5bd2-9d8a-eb4a1a9596c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681021} {:answer/id #uuid "40a91c47-a75b-5942-abd7-a9c48863945a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681036} {:answer/id #uuid "92cc6b64-64e7-56d6-bd62-9970b0591728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681062} {:answer/id #uuid "c279152d-99f2-5dc8-8695-c34924b35627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92cc6b64-64e7-56d6-bd62-9970b0591728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y^2