:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5820c9fa-675d-5965-97e0-7a47c465d7df" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor for\n\n$$\ny^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x+2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0,\\qquad y>0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options to complete the reasoning.\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\ndepends only on $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nHere $\\exp(u)=e^u$.\n\nHere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=y^3,\\qquad Q(x,y)=2xy\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-3}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)={{field-5}}\n$$\n\ndepends only on $y$. Integrating its negative gives\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the original equation gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "For a factor depending only on $y$, exactness requires $I\\,P_y+I'P=I\\,Q_x$. Thus $I'/I=-(P_y-Q_x)/P$, giving\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=3y^2,\\qquad Q_x=2y \\\\ h(y)&=\\frac{3y^2-2y}{y^3}=\\frac{3}{y}-\\frac{2}{y^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis ratio is independent of $x$. Since $y>0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y)&=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\left(\\frac{3}{y}-\\frac{2}{y^2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-3\\ln y-\\frac{2}{y}\\right) \\\\ &=y^{-3}e^{-2/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe arbitrary nonzero constant multiplier can be chosen as $1$. Multiplication yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&(y^{-3}e^{-2/y})\\, [\\\\ &\\quad y^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x+2xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y] =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\ne^{-2/y}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+2xy^{-2}e^{-2/y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nAs a check, both cross-partials are $2y^{-2}e^{-2/y}$, so this equation is exact."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 