\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8081adaf-8dae-52ca-a16e-f66fa2de92b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\ne\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681302} :answer-field/id #uuid "e064ccd7-fc08-50d1-9458-f823aa0b8962" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "168eddf1-79d4-59fe-9942-5c3f5bd75045" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation, on a region where $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$.\n\n$$\ny^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x y}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo test this candidate, multiply both coefficients and check whether the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{y}{x},\\qquad Q=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-2}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x y}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A candidate is an integrating factor if multiplying the equation by it gives an exact equation. First multiply both coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x y}\\left(y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)&=0 \\\\ \\frac{y}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x - \\frac{x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the transformed equation,\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{y}{x},\\qquad Q=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe exactness test compares the partial derivatives as functions on the region:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(-\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\ne\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation isn't exact. Therefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x y}$ isn't an integrating factor for the initial equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "251a0c04-91da-5639-9a68-470f555cd68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\ne\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681263} {:answer/id #uuid "87afdc84-c601-5165-b6df-3a69a1e0a107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87afdc84-c601-5165-b6df-3a69a1e0a107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681306} :answer-field/id #uuid "1606eda8-83b3-557c-9bba-8ceeab0ec1fb" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681255} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025019ec-f09b-5580-915f-de8be7883655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681247} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea950fa-d64e-5db5-849d-7c298b96eccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{x^{2} y^{3}}" :db/id