 #edb/ref 17592186681281} {:answer/id #uuid "5a14593a-30b8-548f-9640-6672062734b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x^{5} y^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681286} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f1f184-5f07-54d6-a9a2-2cfa59711567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\frac{2}{x y^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681323} {:answer/id #uuid "b008550d-4060-54b4-99b8-6f6a923c4b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x - 2 x^{5} y^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7f1f184-5f07-54d6-a9a2-2cfa59711567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\frac{2}{x y^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681323} :answer-field/id #uuid "e61d5a66-7be1-58bb-99fd-064f2a44c6b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4265516c-1434-5ba2-b392-2e3c7afe5da3" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation, on a region where $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$.\n\n$$\nx y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x^{3} y^{4}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo test this candidate, multiply both coefficients and check whether the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}},\\qquad Q=\\frac{2}{x y^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-2}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x^{3} y^{4}}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A candidate is an integrating factor if multiplying the equation by it gives an exact equation. First multiply both coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x^{3} y^{4}}\\left(x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + 2 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)&=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\frac{2}{x y^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the transformed equation,\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}},\\qquad Q=\\frac{2}{x y^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe exactness test compares the partial derivatives as functions on the region:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\frac{1}{x^{2} y^{2}}\\right)=-\\frac{2}{x^{2} y^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\frac{2}{x y^{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{2}{x^{2} y^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation is exact. Therefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x^{3} y^{4}}$ is an integrating factor for the initial equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "685575ad-7495-56de-940e-48e23eb35fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681292} {:answer/id #uuid "afac8aa1-2e55-5383-ba62-1659beba98d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\ne\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afac8aa1-2e55-5383-ba62-1659beba98d5" :a