bc" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation, on a region where $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$.\n\n$$\n7 y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + 2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=x^{2}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo test this candidate, multiply both coefficients and check whether the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let\n\n$$\nP=7 x^{2} y,\\qquad Q=2 x^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-2}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=x^{2}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A candidate is an integrating factor if multiplying the equation by it gives an exact equation. First multiply both coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}\\left(7 y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + 2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)&=0 \\\\ 7 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + 2 x^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the transformed equation,\n\n$$\nP=7 x^{2} y,\\qquad Q=2 x^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe exactness test compares the partial derivatives as functions on the region:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(7 x^{2} y\\right)=7 x^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x^{3}\\right)=6 x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\ne\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation isn't exact. Therefore, $I(x,y)=x^{2}$ isn't an integrating factor for the initial equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12773cc5-cbee-5bd7-9465-81e84f3d05da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\fra