}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eff0eb98-be8f-5ddb-a224-ebd07cc4911c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681364} :answer-field/id #uuid "f56ac45e-ba7d-507a-8d60-609f1d02e011" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2c4d92c-5194-555d-ae9f-30b250d7fe8e" :question/problem "Consider the following inexact differential equation, on a region where $x\\ne0$ and $y\\ne0$.\n\n$$\n-4 x y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + 6 x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below about whether $I(x,y)=\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{4}}$ is an integrating factor of this equation.\n\nTo test this candidate, multiply both coefficients and check whether the following equation is exact:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=0\n$$\n\nFor this equation, let\n\n$$\nP=-\\frac{4 y^{3}}{x^{3}},\\qquad Q=\\frac{6 y^{2}}{x^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-2}},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{{field-4}},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation {{field-5}} exact.\n\nTherefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{4}}$ {{field-6}} an integrating factor for the initial differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A candidate is an integrating factor if multiplying the equation by it gives an exact equation. First multiply both coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{4}}\\left(-4 x y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + 6 x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)&=0 \\\\ -\\frac{4 y^{3}}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x + \\frac{6 y^{2}}{x^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the transformed equation,\n\n$$\nP=-\\frac{4 y^{3}}{x^{3}},\\qquad Q=\\frac{6 y^{2}}{x^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe exactness test compares the partial derivatives as functions on the region:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\frac{4 y^{3}}{x^{3}}\\right)=-\\frac{12 y^{2}}{x^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\frac{6 y^{2}}{x^{2}}\\right)=-\\frac{12 y^{2}}{x^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x},\n$$\n\nthe transformed equation is exact. Therefore, $I(x,y)=\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{4}}$ is an integrating factor for the initial equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6d347a-7aef-500a-8e6a-e8e52b0d5011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- 3 x^{2}}((6 x y + 7 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681436} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbde8a0-715c-528f-bfae-b86a4a156d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3 x^{2}}((6 x y + 7 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681458} {:answer/id #uuid "7de8d8ab-6b03-58e7-9ed5-e76cfa5c1fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{- \\frac{3 x^{2}}{2}}((6 x y + 7 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681475} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef8901a-eaa9-5a43-a204-c9bf6c999bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{\\frac{3 x^{2}}{2}}((6 x y + 7 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dbde8a0-715c-528f-bfae-b86a4a156d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3 x^{2}}((6 x y + 7 x)\\,\\text{d}x+\\,\\text{d}y)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681458} :answer-field/id #uuid "21ff70f6-86fa-5510-b2ee-107970b4aa05" :answer-field/ke