r.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681513} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8c92ee-4def-5a03-b135-2441617a773b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1766d18-5135-5a8e-bf4d-e2edbbcd0c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681510} :answer-field/id #uuid "f625bd36-c935-5d4e-a683-e611935762f9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff416bf9-988a-577d-90f2-5b5cdb25e21b" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find an **integrating factor** to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n5 xy'=- 7 x^{2} - 15 y\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. Work on the interval $x>0$.\n\nFor a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes $e^x$.\n\nFirst, rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=7 x^{2} + 15 y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=5 x\n$$\n\nNow compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-7}}\n$$\n\nand multiplication gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$.\n\nHere $\\exp(x)=e^x$.\n\nFirst, rewrite our equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n(7 x^{2} + 15 y)\\,\\text{d}x+5 x\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=7 x^{2} + 15 y,\\qquad Q(x,y)=5 x\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(7 x^{2} + 15 y)=15 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(5 x)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x})&=\\frac{1}{5 x}(15-(5)) \\\\ &=\\frac{2}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a function of $x$ only. Choose the multiplicative constant to be one. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)&=\\exp(\\int \\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\ &=\\exp(2 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &=x^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn $x>0$, the power rule for logarithms gives $\\exp(k\\ln x)=x^k$.\n\nMultiply the original differential equation by this integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x)((7 x^{2} + 15 y)\\,\\text{d}x+5 x\\,\\text{d}y)&=I(x)\\cdot 0 \\\\ x^{2}((7 x^{2} + 15 y)\\,\\text{d}x+5 x\\,\\text{d}y)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681717 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67e3e4a1-102a-5348-9cdd-907a37ce4c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{Q}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681745} {:answer/id #uuid "6df08393-53c9-5a12-9d96-73f60c2f19af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681750} {:answ