eft(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681702} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcb97ba6-fc17-54dd-9934-ef07037f6212" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c1c6d5d-5494-506d-8f4c-be3036c659bc" :question/problem "Find an integrating factor to convert the following differential equation into an **exact equation**. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$$\n\\left(3 y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0,\\qquad y>0\n$$\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nHere $\\exp(y)$ denotes $e^y$.\n\nDenote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3 y^{3},\\qquad Q(x,y)=- 2 x y\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\nFor this equation,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nThis depends on $y$ only, so\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nMultiplying gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "For an equation in differential form, the condition is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=h(y)\n$$\n\nwith $h(y)$ independent of $x$. Then\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)\n$$\n\nSet\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=3 y^{3},\\qquad Q(x,y)=- 2 x y\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(3 y^{3}\\right)=9 y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- 2 x y\\right)=- 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{3 y^{3}}\\left[\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\right] \\\\ &=\\frac{9 y + 2}{3 y^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeparating the powers gives\n\n$$\nh(y)=\\frac{3}{y} + \\frac{2}{3 y^{2}}\n$$\n\nThe ratio depends on $y$ only. On the stated domain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y)&=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\left(\\frac{3}{y} + \\frac{2}{3 y^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-\\left(3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)} - \\frac{2}{3 y}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-3\\ln y\\right)\\exp\\left(\\frac{2}{3 y}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{e^{\\frac{2}{3 y}}}{y^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe choose the arbitrary nonzero constant multiplier to be $1$. Here $y>0$ permits the logarithm and exponential conversion. Multiplying the original equation by this factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\left(\\frac{e^{\\frac{2}{3 y}}}{y^{3}}\\right)\\, [\\\\ &\\quad \\left(3 y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186681786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75efdf49-51e6-5965-bd8d-4248ab64db35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{\\frac{2}{y}}}{y^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681755} {:answer/id #uuid "86e865f4-a11a-5f49-b121-f031dbc54205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{\\frac{2}{y}}}{y^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681763} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2859ff-9fef-5d1d-8fde-c115dce79df8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{- \\frac{2}{y}}}{y^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186681816} {:answer/id #uuid "df4fd736-de95-5500-846a-f885c34d422c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident