 only, so\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-7}}\n$$\n\nMultiplying gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "For an equation in differential form, the condition is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=h(y)\n$$\n\nwith $h(y)$ independent of $x$. Then\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)\n$$\n\nMultiplying the derivative equation by $\\mathrm{d}x$ and moving its right side to the left gives\n\n$$\n\\left(x \\left(2 y^{2} + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSet\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x \\left(2 y^{2} + 4\\right),\\qquad Q(x,y)=2 y\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x \\left(2 y^{2} + 4\\right)\\right)=4 x y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 y\\right)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{x \\left(2 y^{2} + 4\\right)}\\left[\\left(4 x y\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right] \\\\ &=\\frac{2 y}{y^{2} + 2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ratio depends on $y$ only. On the stated domain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y)&=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\left(\\frac{2 y}{y^{2} + 2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-\\left(\\ln{\\left(2 y^{2} + 4 \\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2 y^{2} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe choose the arbitrary nonzero constant multiplier to be $1$. Here $y>0$ permits the logarithm and exponential conversion. Multiplying the original equation by this factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\left(\\frac{1}{2 y^{2} + 4}\\right)\\, [\\\\ &\\quad \\left(x \\left(2 y^{2} + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [