ing factor to convert the following differential equation into an **exact equation**. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$$\n2 y^{2}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=-\\left(x \\left(3 y^{3} + 4\\right)\\right),\\qquad x>0,\\ y>0\n$$\n\nFor an equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nHere $\\exp(y)$ denotes $e^y$.\n\nFirst rewrite the equation in differential form:\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$\n\nLet $P$ and $Q$ be the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ in that form.\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\n\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}={{field-5}}\n$$\n\nFor this equation,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nThis depends on $y$ only, so\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-7}}\n$$\n\nMultiplying gives the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "For an equation in differential form, the condition is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=h(y)\n$$\n\nwith $h(y)$ independent of $x$. Then\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\exp\\left(-\\int h(y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right)\n$$\n\nMultiplying the derivative equation by $\\mathrm{d}x$ and moving its right side to the left gives\n\n$$\n\\left(x \\left(3 y^{3} + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\n$$\n\nSet\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=x \\left(3 y^{3} + 4\\right),\\qquad Q(x,y)=2 y^{2}\n$$\n\nCompute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x \\left(3 y^{3} + 4\\right)\\right)=9 x y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 y^{2}\\right)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{x \\left(3 y^{3} + 4\\right)}\\left[\\left(9 x y^{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right] \\\\ &=\\frac{9 y^{2}}{3 y^{3} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ratio depends on $y$ only. On the stated domain,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y)&=\\exp\\left(-\\int\\left(\\frac{9 y^{2}}{3 y^{3} + 4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\right) \\\\ &=\\exp\\left(-\\left(\\ln{\\left(3 y^{3} + 4 \\right)}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3 y^{3} + 4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe choose the arbitrary nonzero constant multiplier to be $1$. Here $y>0$ permits the logarithm and exponential conversion. Multiplying the original equation by this factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\left(\\frac{1}{3 y^{3} + 4}\\right)\\, [\\\\ &\\quad \\left(x \\left(3 y^{3} + 4\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186690148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c465b3b1-8451-5f1e-a37c-6ea1744dc2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\left(x \\left(3 y^{4} + 2\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &{}+\\left(y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690131} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ee00b5-cfd5-52ad-bedc-c2dda7a964ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\left(y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &{}+\\left(x \\left(3 y^{4} + 2\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690141} {:answer/id #uuid "ec34a4ae-a4cb-5413-b3f1-ef985fd2be50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{aligned}&\\left(3 y^{4} + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &{}+\\left(x y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=0\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186690149} {:answer/id #uuid "ff60b3f1-91b2-540d-8097-9467d6bd0ade